api.github.com/users/jklaise/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jklaise/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jklaise/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2022-09-30T14:22:29Z","updated_at":"2022-09-30T14:37:42Z","body":"Perhaps not the right place to ask, but I've failed to find the accepted way of defining _literal collections_. I.e. if I want a constant list `[\"foo\", \"bar\"]` I could write it like this:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nclass Model(BaseModel):\r\n    literal_list = Field([\"foo\", \"bar\"], const=True)\r\n```\r\n(Note that the original problem still persists, but at least we can define this - though not clear how to type this).\r\n\r\nWhereas if `const` is deprecated in favour of `Literal` then it's not clear to me how to achieve the above as `Literal` can't be used to specify literal collections afaik.\r\n\r\nEDIT: To partially answer my own question, if we're only concerned with the resulting `json` or the schema, then using tuples works:\r\n```python\r\nclass Model(BaseModel):\r\n    literal_json_list: Tuple[Literal[\"foo\"], Literal[\"bar\"]]\r\n```\r\nHowever, on the Python side this would not be sufficient if we actually want a list not a tuple.","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pydantic/pydantic/issues/comments/1263642336/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"jklaise","id":13080878,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzMDgwODc4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13080878?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jklaise","html_url":"https://github.com/jklaise","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jklaise/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jklaise/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jklaise/gi